n as a compile dependency, the generated scaffold gets OAuth-flow HTTP mocking for free, with zero extra Maven coordinates.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nFindings from research:\n- **POM (1.35.0)**: compile deps are `google-http-client`, `google-http-client-gson`, and `guava` — all pure-Java, already well-represented elsewhere in this metadata repo with no special setup.\n- **Contrast case**: `com.google.auth:google-auth-library-oauth2-http` (a *different* library, despite the similar name) needed an extra `com.google.appengine:appengine-api-1.0-sdk` test dependency because it has real App Engine classes. `google-oauth-client`'s jar contents show no such AppEngine/servlet coupling — just `oauth2`, `oauth` (1.0a signing), and `openidconnect` packages.\n- **Crypto**: `OAuthHmacSigner`/`OAuthRsaSigner` use standard `javax.crypto`/`java.security` JCA primitives (HMAC-SHA1/256, RSA) — no custom provider or keystore needed under Native Image.\n- **No network/Docker need**: OAuth flow tests (`AuthorizationCodeFlow`, `Credential`, token exchange) can be fully exercised against a `MockHttpTransport` returning canned JSON token responses, exactly the pattern already used for `google-api-services-sqladmin`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"no_action\",\n  \"summary\": \"Pure-Java OAuth helper library; compile deps (google-http-client, google-http-client-gson, guava) are already standard in this repo, and google-http-client transitively brings MockHttpTransport for mocking OAuth token exchanges without network/Docker. No AppEngine/servlet coupling (unlike the similarly-named google-auth-library-oauth2-http) and no special crypto/env setup needed for HMAC/RSA signing.\",\n  \"deterministic_setup\": [],\n  \"agent_guidance\": \"Use com.google.api.client.testing.http.MockHttpTransport/MockLowLevelHttpResponse (bundled in google-http-client, already a transitive dependency) to stub token-endpoint responses when testing AuthorizationCodeFlow/TokenRequest/Cred